{"title":"2006 iTest Problems/Problem 37","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_iTest_Problems/Problem_37&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>To begin, let's number the equations as follows\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/9/c096711377df29f0b5dfabc29a188579adf95336.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align}  x^2 + xy + y^2 &amp;= 1 \\\\ y^2 + yz +z^2 &amp;= 2 \\\\ z^2 + zx +x^2 &amp;= 3 \\end{align}\" width=\"304\" height=\"74\" />\n</p><p>In order to solve for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/a/f5a3faa3658bcbd8bde8e16cfa5baf019619fc9c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"15\" height=\"18\" />, we begin by subtracting the first equation from the second\nequation:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/8/388c59b8528165825e84366ca769182c74c40069.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}  1&amp;=y^2 + yz +z^2 -(x^2 + xy + y^2)\\\\ &amp;= z^2 + yz - xy - x^2 \\\\ &amp;=(z-x)(x + y + z) \\\\ \\end{align*}\" width=\"263\" height=\"72\" />\n</p><p>Similarily, subtracting the second eqaution from the  third equation:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/d/15de2157e8d55fc1612bd161b0f0406c3896254e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}  1&amp;= x^2 + xz +z^2 -(y^2 + zy + z^2)\\\\ &amp;= x^2 + xz - zy - y^2 \\\\ &amp;=(x-y)(x + y + z) \\\\ \\end{align*}\" width=\"262\" height=\"72\" />\n</p><p>Subtracting the factored equations we see that\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/f/47f10ac1438a41b9272fbc79809f6552fefb91c3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}  0&amp;=(x-y)(x + y + z)-(z-x)(x + y + z)\\\\ &amp;=(x+y+z)((x-y)-(z-x))\\\\ &amp;=(x+y+z)(2x-y-z)\\\\ \\end{align*}\" width=\"341\" height=\"68\" />\n</p><p>Therefore, we can conclude that either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/2/10248f0874e575270f3c6fcf7abb1fed45f10ed8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y+z=0 \\Rightarrow x=-z-y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"227\" height=\"17\" />, or that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/1/e019d532d6fd087871eedf49ebaf0820eddbea55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2x-y-z=0 \\Rightarrow x=\\frac{z+y}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"222\" height=\"35\" />.  As we are told that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/a/f5a3faa3658bcbd8bde8e16cfa5baf019619fc9c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"15\" height=\"18\" /> has a unique value, we can use either option.\n</p><p>Continuing with the second option, we can plug <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/2/972e3adaf9e22a26be5672f35c1198b049171065.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=\\frac{z+y}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"78\" height=\"35\" /> into the first equation:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/0/d803e329b04599a11b08af3548b3fe6537e924b6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}  1&amp;=x^2+xy+y^2\\\\ &amp;=\\left(\\frac{z+y}{2}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{z+y}{2}\\right)\\cdot y +y^2 \\\\ &amp;=\\frac{z^2+2yz+y^2}{4} + \\frac{yz+y^2}{2} + y^2 \\end{align*}\" width=\"264\" height=\"120\" />\n</p><p>Multiplying each side of the resulting equation by four:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/d/fbd8eb9dcfa373869ef8f7e5ed6d760df38151e4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}  4&amp;=4 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{z^2+2yz+y^2}{4} + \\frac{yz+y^2}{2} + y^2 \\right)\\\\ &amp;=(z^2+2yz+y^2)+(2yz+2y^2)+4y^2\\\\ &amp;=z^2+4yz+7y^2 \\end{align*}\" width=\"312\" height=\"96\" />\n</p><p>We can then subtract twice our original second equation, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/6/9c6e5510e495b6f256ac1402df816e4db2120cad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y^2+yz+z^2=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"129\" height=\"18\" />, from the equation from the previous step, yielding:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/0/b30717c131aabbd57298abf67c552e05a09d3d0c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}  4-2\\cdot2&amp;=(z^2+4yz+7y^2)-2(y^2+yz+z^2)\\\\ 0&amp;=5y^2+2yz-z^2\\\\ \\end{align*}\" width=\"356\" height=\"46\" />\n</p><p>Rearranging the new equation, we see that:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/a/54ad3caf63537370b812ebbb99146c85aa9e6ef1.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}  5y^2 &amp;=z^2-2yz\\\\ 6y^2 &amp;= z^2-2yz+y^2\\\\ 6y^2 &amp;=(z-y)^2\\\\ \\sqrt{6y^2}&amp;=\\sqrt{(z-y)^2}\\\\ y\\sqrt{6}&amp;=z-y\\\\ \\end{align*}\" width=\"171\" height=\"143\" />\n</p><p>We can ignore the negative root of the RHS because both y and z are positive, and because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/2/c320c71619dcd524877e17a292ef54037dc3c4e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z&gt;y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"15\" /> which is a result of (3) being greater than (1).\nContinuing to manipulate the newest equation yields:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/6/f06fe85896e7449cbb6f8fc22d8308d946ac5b0d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}  z-y &amp;=y\\sqrt{6}\\\\ z&amp;=y\\sqrt{6}+y\\\\ z&amp;=y(\\sqrt{6}+1) \\end{align*}\" width=\"142\" height=\"81\" />\n</p><p>Plugging this back into (2)\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/1/3f1833d96806970f3a52624624564326757f8de2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}  y^2 + yz +z^2 =&amp; \\quad 2\\\\ y^2+y \\cdot y(\\sqrt{6}+1) + (y(\\sqrt{6}+1))^2 =&amp;\\\\ y^2\\cdot(1+(\\sqrt{6}+1)+(\\sqrt{6}+1)^2)=&amp;\\\\ y^2\\cdot(1+(\\sqrt{6}+1)+(6+2\\sqrt{6}+1))=&amp;\\\\ y^2\\cdot(9+3\\sqrt{6})=&amp; \\end{align*}\" width=\"321\" height=\"139\" />\n</p><p>Solving for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/a/f5a3faa3658bcbd8bde8e16cfa5baf019619fc9c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"15\" height=\"18\" /> yields that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/5/8e5a83e0cf6bbfa451a418c73674a6d0ce6739ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y^2= \\frac{2}{9+3\\sqrt{6}} \\Rightarrow y^2=\\frac{2}{9+3\\sqrt{6}} \\cdot \\frac{9-3\\sqrt{6}}{9-3\\sqrt{6}} = \\frac{18-6\\sqrt{6}}{27} = \\frac{6-2\\sqrt{6}}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"523\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p>Therefore, the solution is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/7/0975f3a6d2dd871a923af9116552a20b656b1d96.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6+2+6+9 = \\boxed{23}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"157\" height=\"23\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_iTest_Problems/Problem_37&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_iTest\" title=\"2006 iTest\">2006 iTest</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_iTest_Problems\" title=\"2006 iTest Problems\">(Problems</a>, <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_iTest_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2006 iTest Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key)</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by:<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2006_iTest_Problems/Problem_36\" title=\"2006 iTest Problems/Problem 36\">Problem 36</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by:<br /><b><a href=\"/wi